\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {How Proposition~\ref {prop:lift} works} (minimum-jerk lift, $T=0.37$\,s). (a) At the no-onset grip $F_{\rm  model}(T)$ the demand never exceeds the certified static capacity and the bound is zero. (b) With 3\% less grip, slip may begin near peak acceleration; if friction does not drop ($r_{\max }=1$) the excess is small and short, and the bound is 0.8\,mm. (c) The same grip with a possible 20\% drop: from the moment slip may begin, the certified kinetic capacity is far below the demand for most of the acceleration phase, and the bound jumps to 23\,mm. This cliff is why slip allowances buy little (Section~\ref {sec:q4}).}}{4}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bound}{{3}{4}{\textbf {How Proposition~\ref {prop:lift} works} (minimum-jerk lift, $T=0.37$\,s). (a) At the no-onset grip $F_{\rm model}(T)$ the demand never exceeds the certified static capacity and the bound is zero. (b) With 3\% less grip, slip may begin near peak acceleration; if friction does not drop ($r_{\max }=1$) the excess is small and short, and the bound is 0.8\,mm. (c) The same grip with a possible 20\% drop: from the moment slip may begin, the certified kinetic capacity is far below the demand for most of the acceleration phase, and the bound jumps to 23\,mm. This cliff is why slip allowances buy little (Section~\ref {sec:q4})}{figure.caption.2}{}}
