\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Anatomy of a failure} (object 2, $r=1.2$, seed 2). Top: demand $g+a(t)$ against the static, kinetic and certified capacities for the grip each policy chose. Middle: speed of the fingers relative to the rod, with kinetic friction active shaded. Bottom: slip. Left, B2: the demand crosses the static capacity at 0.03\,s; the rod breaks away at 0.09\,s, kinetic friction persists for 0.17\,s, and slip ends at 21\,mm. Right, B5: the certified grip keeps the demand below the static capacity throughout; the residual motion is elastic and returns to zero.}}{7}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:anatomy}{{5}{7}{\textbf {Anatomy of a failure} (object 2, $r=1.2$, seed 2). Top: demand $g+a(t)$ against the static, kinetic and certified capacities for the grip each policy chose. Middle: speed of the fingers relative to the rod, with kinetic friction active shaded. Bottom: slip. Left, B2: the demand crosses the static capacity at 0.03\,s; the rod breaks away at 0.09\,s, kinetic friction persists for 0.17\,s, and slip ends at 21\,mm. Right, B5: the certified grip keeps the demand below the static capacity throughout; the residual motion is elastic and returns to zero}{figure.caption.7}{}}
