\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Training curves.} Reward of the current (unperturbed) policy on each iteration's 6 training objects. Both methods improve on the model controller (dotted) within about 600 episodes. The unshielded runs dip whenever an iteration's batch contains a lift that slips: three of the five B2 runs ended on such a batch. The shielded runs never fail and converge to the same reward, about $-0.50$.}}{17}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:training}{{8}{17}{\textbf {Training curves.} Reward of the current (unperturbed) policy on each iteration's 6 training objects. Both methods improve on the model controller (dotted) within about 600 episodes. The unshielded runs dip whenever an iteration's batch contains a lift that slips: three of the five B2 runs ended on such a batch. The shielded runs never fail and converge to the same reward, about $-0.50$}{figure.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Test set, every method at every $r$} ($r_{\max }=1.2$). Failure counts with Wilson 95\% intervals (\%); $\Delta T$ and $\Delta F/mg$ against B0 with hierarchical bootstrap 95\% intervals (seeds, then objects); ``obj.\ worse'' counts objects that failed at least once under the method but never under B0, with the Holm-corrected exact McNemar $p$ (B0S and B4t are outside the pre-registered Holm families and show the raw $p$). All lift-time and grip differences have Holm-corrected Wilcoxon $p<10^{-10}$.}}{18}{table.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
